# PHASE 1 — RESTORE THE CAMERA AND SCROLL EXPERIENCE

This is the highest-priority repair.

The previous version had a better camera angle, a clearer view of each planet, and information cards that appeared at the appropriate time. The current version has degraded these behaviors.

## 1.1 Camera composition

Restore a deliberate, cinematic viewing angle for every featured planet.

Requirements:

- The planet should be the visual subject of its section.
- The camera must frame the planet at an appropriate distance and angle.
- The planet must not be clipped by the viewport.
- The planet must not disappear behind an information card.
- The camera should not point at empty space while the card displays information about an object that is not visible.
- Preserve the smoothness and composition of the earlier camera journey where the original implementation can be recovered.
- Do not apply one arbitrary camera angle to every planet.
- Allow different framing for the Sun, rocky planets, gas giants, rings, and moon exploration.
- Recalculate camera targets and framing when the viewport changes.

## 1.2 Synchronize scroll progress, camera movement, and information cards

The camera and information cards must be coordinated by the same active-section or transition state.

When a section becomes active:

1. Begin the camera transition toward the featured object.
2. Reveal that object's information card at the beginning of the relevant transition, when the object is becoming visible.
3. Ensure the card and camera correspond to the same object.
4. Finish the transition with the object framed clearly and the card fully readable.

Do not wait until the entire section has finished scrolling before showing the card.

Do not display the previous planet's information while the camera is already focusing on the next planet.

Avoid flickering, delayed cards, duplicate transitions, abrupt camera jumps, and conflicting scroll triggers.

Handle rapid scrolling, reverse scrolling, manual selection, window resizing, and returning from moon or interior exploration.

If the project uses GSAP ScrollTrigger, inspect the existing trigger start/end values, timelines, refresh behavior, and cleanup logic before changing them.

Do not introduce a second competing camera animation system.

## 1.3 Preserve the existing solar-system journey

The main 3D scene must remain persistent throughout the scroll experience.

- Planetary rotation continues while the scene is active.
- Scrolling produces meaningful camera travel through the solar system.
- Each major section has an intentional visual composition.
- Planet selection and moon selection do not permanently break scroll navigation.
- Returning from a focused object restores the expected solar-system context.
- The existing educational sections, comparison content, quiz, navigation, and language controls remain available.

